# Product Requirements Document
## Household Ledger: What You Own, What It Cost, What Protects It

**Status:** Draft (revised)
**Product:** Existing Home Inventory & Personal Finance Application
**Feature:** Household Ledger — Onboarding & Core Model
**Priority:** High

**Revision note:** This is a scope-reduced rewrite of the original "Intelligent Home & Household Onboarding" draft. It keeps the household data model and onboarding shape, cuts kitchen/food, full home schematics, the full home-systems taxonomy, and most document types, and elevates the transaction↔item↔document connection from a buried "long-term" aside to the product's actual thesis. See §6 for what was removed and why.

---

## 1. Overview

The application currently provides two capabilities:

- AI-assisted home inventory management
- Personal finance and expense tracking

Today they don't know about each other. An inventory system knows:

> Cordless Drill → Garage → Bin 12

A finance system independently knows:

> Home Depot → $149.99

Neither knows that the transaction *bought* the drill, that the drill has an owner, a location, a receipt, and — where it applies — a warranty.

This feature introduces a **Household Ledger**: a structure that connects people, physical spaces, belongings, and the money and paperwork behind them, so those questions can be answered without digging through email, a shoebox of receipts, or memory.

The product model is:

**People + Places + Things + Money + Proof (receipts, warranties, manuals)**

Food, full home systems, and home schematics are explicitly out of this model for now — see §6.

The near-term promise, and the one onboarding should teach in the first two minutes:

> **Never lose track of what you own, what it cost, or whether it's still protected.**

"Ask your home anything" remains the long-term aspiration (§41) but is not a near-term design driver — it only becomes true once the data underneath it is real. Treat it as a north star to grow into, not a justification for building broadly now.

---

## 2. Problem

An inventory record and a finance record describe the same object from two directions and never meet:

- the transaction purchased the item,
- the item belongs to a household member,
- it lives in a particular part of the home,
- its receipt and warranty relate to the same object,
- future maintenance or replacement cost should trace back to it.

Separately, household members may exist without application accounts — children in particular — and the product needs to represent them without requiring a login.

The product needs a household model that connects these entities, scoped tightly enough that a small team can actually build and maintain it.

---

## 3. Product Vision

Create a persistent record of what a household owns, what it cost, where it is, and what protects it.

**WHO** — who lives in the household and who owns or uses things.
**WHAT** — what the household owns.
**WHERE** — where things are physically located.
**WHEN / HOW MUCH** — when things were purchased and what they cost.
**PROOF** — receipts, warranties, and manuals for what was purchased.
**KNOWLEDGE** — search and AI reasoning across all of the above.

The goal is not a home-inventory database and not a personal-finance app bolted to one. It's the connective layer between them — the thing neither one can be on its own.

---

## 4. Goals

### Primary Goals

1. Establish household structure (household, home, spaces) with minimal upfront configuration.
2. Represent household members independently of application accounts.
3. Let inventory belong to individuals or the household.
4. Deliver a working AI-powered first-use experience in under two minutes.
5. Connect financial transactions to the physical items they purchased.
6. Avoid overwhelming users with configuration before they've seen value.

### Secondary Goals (progressive, post-onboarding)

- Appliance recognition and warranty lookup
- Receipts, warranties, and manuals as searchable documents
- A simple map of critical home locations (shutoffs, panel, router)
- Household search across people, places, things, and money

---

## 5. Non-Goals

Initial onboarding will **not** require users to:

- catalog their entire home
- manually enter every possession
- connect financial accounts
- configure maintenance schedules
- upload warranties
- invite every household member
- categorize every item manually

These are introduced progressively, after the user has already seen value.

---

## 6. Explicitly Out of Scope

These were in the original draft and are cut, not phased. Re-adding any of them should require a new decision, not a default resumption of the roadmap.

| Cut | Why |
|---|---|
| **Kitchen / fridge / pantry scanning, recipe suggestions** | Computer vision can't sustain the cadence food actually needs (occlusion, no visibility into opened packaging, no reliable quantity or expiration-date reading), the inventory goes stale within a day, and recipe-from-ingredients is already commoditized by every recipe app and general chat AI. Wrong food scans are also the fastest way to make users distrust the *rest* of the app. |
| **Full home schematics / digital twin** (floor plans, room geometry, full electrical/plumbing/HVAC/network as typed infrastructure) | That's a different product, aimed at renovators and professionals (Matterport, iGuide territory), not a consumer photo app. Consumer willingness to do the capture work is low outside an active renovation. |
| **Home Systems as a rich, typed taxonomy** (separate schemas per electrical/plumbing/HVAC/safety/network category) | Replaced with a much smaller concept: a handful of pinned critical locations (§30). No per-category data model. |
| **Fridge-diff reconciliation** (comparing old/new fridge photos to infer consumption) | Falls with kitchen scanning above; it's a hard image-alignment problem in service of a feature that isn't shipping. |
| **Full document taxonomy** (insurance, HOA, inspections, contractor invoices, utility/property records) | Narrowed to receipts, warranties, and manuals (§29) — the three types that directly answer "what did I pay, and is it covered." The rest require much harder parsing for much lower query frequency. |
| **Universal Scan across all classification types on day one** | Launches with two modes (§32), not seven. Auto-classification expands only as each underlying workflow proves reliable. |

