## 22. Progressive Onboarding & Contextual Household Members

Household members are **not** collected as a dedicated onboarding step. They're introduced the first time they're actually needed:

The first time a user answers "Who does this belong to?" with anyone other than themselves, offer **+ Add someone** inline. Fields: Name, Relationship (Partner/Spouse, Child, Parent, Family Member, Roommate, Other), optional photo, then:

**Will this person use the app?**
**Not right now** → managed profile (§24) · **Invite to the app** → sends an invitation

This removes the single biggest abandonment point identified in review — asking a brand-new user to enumerate their family before they've seen any value.

---

## 23. Managed Profiles

No email, phone, password, or authentication account required. A managed profile can still have belongings, expenses, documents, and responsibilities attached to it, and can later be linked to a real Account with all historical relationships intact.

**Open question to resolve before this holds years of data:** when a managed child profile's real person turns 18 and wants their own account, does their history transfer to them, and does the parent lose visibility at that point? This is a consent and identity decision, not an engineering one — resolve it before managed profiles have years of data attached, not after.

---

## 24. Finance Setup

Not mandatory during onboarding.

**Understand where your household money goes**
Connect your accounts to track spending, bills, and purchases automatically.

CTA: **Connect Account**

